.TH LINUXSCP 1 "2026-10-12" "LinuxSCP 1.0" "LinuxSCP"
.SH NAME
linuxscp \- secure file transfer client for the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linuxscp
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ sftp:// [ user [: password ]@] host [: port ]/ path ]
.br
.B linuxscp
.B \-batch
.BI \-script= file
.br
.B linuxscp
.B \-batch
.BI \-command= commands
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linuxscp
is the console file transfer client of LinuxSCP. It connects to an SFTP
server over SSH, and transfers files to and from the local file system,
either from a script, from a command line, or interactively.
.PP
The SSH and SFTP protocol code is the PuTTY core, driven by a POSIX front
end.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-script= file
Read the commands to run from
.IR file ,
one command per line. Use
.B \-
to read from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-command= commands
Run
.IR commands .
Several commands may be given, separated by
.BR ; .
.TP
.BI \-hostkey= fingerprint
Accept only the host key with this fingerprint. Use
.B *
to accept any key for this connection only; it is not added to the persistent
host-key cache. Without this option an unknown host key is accepted only
after the client has been able to ask; in
.B \-batch
mode the connection fails instead.
.TP
.BI \-privatekey= file
Private key to authenticate with. Both PuTTY
.B .ppk
files and OpenSSH/ssh.com keys (including the OpenSSH new format used by
.IR id_rsa " and " id_ed25519 )
are accepted; a key in a foreign format is imported for the connection. An
encrypted key asks for its passphrase interactively and cannot be used in
batch mode: load such a key into an ssh-agent instead.
.TP
.BI \-username= name
User name to authenticate with.
.TP
.BI \-password= password
Password to authenticate with. The
.B LINUXSCP_PASSWORD
environment variable is used when this option is not given, which keeps the
password out of the command line.
.TP
.BI \-port= number
Port to connect to (1 through 65535). The default is 22.
.TP
.BI \-timeout= seconds
Positive connection timeout in seconds. The default is 15 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-log= file
Write the session log to
.IR file .
.TP
.B \-batch
Never ask a question. Operations that require an answer fail instead. To
disable overwrite and delete confirmations in a trusted automation script,
run
.B option confirm off
before those operations.
.TP
.B \-gui
Launch the graphical Commander desktop interface.
.TP
.B \-verbose
Print what the SSH connection layer does as it happens.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-help
Print the usage and exit.
.SH SITES
A site is a URL of the form
.PP
.RS
.BI sftp:// [ user [: password ]@] host [: port ]/ path
.RE
.PP
The schemes
.BR sftp:// ,
.BR ssh:// ,
.BR scp:// ,
.BR ftp:// ,
.BR ftps:// ,
.BR dav:// ,
.B webdav://
and
.B s3://
are all accepted, as is a bare
.IR host [ :port ].
The path is the directory the client changes to after connecting. The
password is optional in the URL; percent-encode reserved characters in URL
credentials and paths. Prefer
.B \-password=
or
.B LINUXSCP_PASSWORD
so that the password is not written into a script file.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI open " site"
Connect to
.IR site .
.TP
.B close
Disconnect.
.TP
.BI cd " directory"
Change the remote directory.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the remote directory.
.TP
.BI ls " [" \-al1 "] [" directory ]
List the remote directory.
.B \-l
is the long format, which is the default;
.B \-1
prints names only;
.B \-a
is accepted and does nothing, as entries starting with a dot are always
listed.
.TP
.BI lcd " directory"
Change the local directory.
.TP
.B lpwd
Print the local directory.
.TP
.BI get " [" options "] remote [" local ]
Download a file, a directory recursively, or every file matching a mask.
Optional switches include
.BR \-r " (recursive directory download),"
.BR \-resume " (resume interrupted transfers at target offset),"
.BR \-delete " (remove remote source after transfer),"
.BR \-neweronly " (skip files that are not newer than the target),"
and
.BR \-preservetime " (preserve timestamps)."
When omitted,
.I local
defaults to the current local directory.
.TP
.BI put " [" options "] local [" remote ]
Upload a file, a directory recursively, or every file matching a mask.
Optional switches include
.BR \-r " (recursive directory upload),"
.BR \-resume " (resume interrupted transfers at target offset),"
.BR \-delete " (remove local source after transfer),"
.BR \-neweronly " (skip files that are not newer than the target),"
and
.BR \-preservetime " (preserve timestamps)."
When omitted,
.I remote
defaults to the current remote directory.
.TP
.BI synchronize " [local|remote|both] [" localdir " [" remotedir ]]"
Synchronize directories in the specified direction.
.B remote
pushes newer local files to the remote server,
.B local
pulls newer remote files to the local machine, and
.B both
synchronizes both directions. Supports the
.B \-delete
flag.
.TP
.BI echo " [" message ]
Print a message to the console output.
.TP
.BI rm " file"
Remove remote files.
.TP
.BI rmdir " directory"
Remove a remote directory.
.TP
.BI mkdir " directory"
Create a remote directory.
.TP
.BI mv " from to"
Move or rename a remote file.
.TP
.BI chmod " permissions file"
Change the permissions of a remote file, given in octal.
.TP
.BI stat " file"
Print the attributes of a remote file.
.TP
.BI option " name value"
Configure session options:
.BR option\ confirm\ on|off
controls overwrite and delete confirmations.
.BR option\ batch\ on|off|abort
controls non-interactive batch mode.
.BR option\ resume\ on|off
controls automatic transfer resumption.
.BR option\ reconnect\ <secs>
and
.BR option\ transfer\ binary|ascii
are also recognized for WinSCP script compatibility.
.TP
.B help
List the commands.
.TP
.B exit
End the session. The aliases
.BR bye ,
.B quit
and
.B end
also work.
.PP
Local and remote paths may contain the wildcards
.B *
and
.BR ? .
.PP
.B call
and
.B show
are rejected: the connection runs the SFTP subsystem only, so there is no
remote shell to run a command in.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
The keys held by a running ssh-agent are offered automatically when
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
is set, which is the simplest way to authenticate with an OpenSSH key.
.TP
.B LINUXSCP_PASSWORD
Password to use when
.B \-password=
is not given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero if every command succeeded, non-zero otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Upload everything in the current directory and list the result:
.PP
.RS
.nf
linuxscp \-batch \-command="open sftp://user@example.com/var/www \\
  \-hostkey=SHA256:abcdef... ; put * . ; ls ; exit"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat > sync.txt <<'EOF'
open sftp://user@example.com/var/www \-hostkey=*
get *.html /var/www/html
exit
EOF
linuxscp \-batch \-script=sync.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The Debian client is an SFTP command-line client. While recursive directory
transfers, directory synchronization, and script compatibility options are
supported, it does not yet implement the SCP protocol, FTP, FTPS, WebDAV,
Amazon S3, local-to-local transfers, resuming interrupted transfers or a
graphical interface.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sftp (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh\-agent (1)
.PP
The Debian port is documented in
.I docs/debian.md
of the source package.
